#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

class Test {
public:
    // Static vector to hold pointers to all instances of Test
    static std::vector<const Test*> allData;

    // Non-static integer field to store data
    int data;

    // Default constructor
    Test(int value) : data(value) {
        // Register this instance in the static vector
        allData.push_back(this);
    }
};

// Initialize the static member variable
std::vector<const Test*> Test::allData;

int main() {
    // Create an instance of Test
    Test instance(42);

    // Access the first element in the static vector
    const Test* firstInstance = Test::allData[0];

    // Print the data field of the first instance
    std::cout << "Data: " << firstInstance->data << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This code defines a `Test` class with a static vector `allData` to hold pointers to all instances of the class. Each instance has a non-static integer field `data`. The constructor initializes `data` and registers the instance in the static vector. In the `main` function, an instance of `Test` is created, and the `data` field of the first element in the static vector is printed.
